{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"2be0db7d513f5711340b33f3ff1ae9fab88733301b0d2d3ddd5b3e9b6168ea19","slug":"pages.m16-case-c-q-1-of-2","location":"/pages/229-m16-case-c-q-1-of-2.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Case C→Q: Comparing Groups","content_includes_title":false,"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"City College of San Francisco","given":"City College","non_dropping_particle":"of","family":"San Francisco"},"name":"City College of San Francisco","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"}],"license":{"content":{"id":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","name":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","CC":true}},"numbering":{"title":{"offset":2}},"thumbnail":"/textbook/build/m05-role-type-cq-d17db4bd130e5ed50dcd488fb6183e48.svg","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"229-m16-case-c-q-1-of-2.md","url":"/textbook/build/229-m16-case-c-q-1-o-bdffd39032e43cbc754f3327740cf5bd.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Learning Objectives","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"uJAUrU1lJ4"}],"key":"hum86Z6bEW"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify and distinguish among cases where use of calculations specific to independent samples, matched pairs, and ANOVA are appropriate.","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"key":"BFgnoweoqQ"}],"key":"Ct9U3naQax"}],"key":"binx6iTo3h"}],"key":"G8HsFPIhhw"}],"class":"note","key":"iXAhCHnZqC"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Recall the role-type classification table framing our discussion on inference about the relationship between two variables.","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"WrXb192vgz"}],"key":"ITX4KDxp8Q"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/textbook/build/m05-role-type-cq-d17db4bd130e5ed50dcd488fb6183e48.svg","alt":"The role-type classification table with the C to Q case highlighted: a categorical explanatory variable paired with a quantitative response variable.","key":"irLppXUTEt","urlSource":"images/gen/m05-role-type-cq.svg"}],"enumerator":"1","key":"bgUB4f1uMj"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We start with case C→Q, where the explanatory variable is categorical and the response variable is quantitative. Recall that in the Exploratory Data Analysis unit, examining the relationship between X and Y in this case amounts, in practice, to comparing the distributions of the (quantitative) response Y for each value (category) of the explanatory X. To do that, we used side-by-side boxplots (each representing the distribution of Y in one of the groups defined by X), and supplemented the display with the corresponding descriptive statistics.","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"ok5EJ6P1f7"}],"key":"s2gBXBwmsT"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"What will we do in inference? To understand the logic, we’ll start with an example and then generalize.","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"RqGpoUE8Ww"}],"key":"AAXh9YodzA"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example: GPA and Year in College","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"In5p9SqiHk"}],"key":"GmGYSWGfSr"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Say that our variable of interest is the GPA of college students in the United States. From the previous module we know that since GPA is quantitative, we do inference on μ, the (population) mean GPA among all U.S. college students. Since this module is about relationships, let’s assume that what we are really interested in is not simply GPA, but the relationship between:","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"key":"rPiWsMwM1B"}],"key":"e0PatbD2kQ"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"X:","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"VQt13WAyNO"}],"key":"LXjssBJSHH"},{"type":"text","value":" year in college (1 = freshman, 2 = sophomore, 3 = junior, 4 = senior), and","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"c0CcNRiAgj"}],"key":"obxbmwnLCd"}],"key":"hfVgwxRNpc"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":25,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Y:","position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":25,"column":1}},"key":"cYyTtKz7k5"}],"key":"mNuFEpzJzB"},{"type":"text","value":" GPA","position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":25,"column":1}},"key":"HXOsNJaXvw"}],"key":"cYJsX7wua3"}],"key":"tfoPvE8NL5"}],"key":"kFAqaXwgu0"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In other words, we want to explore whether GPA is related to year in college. The way to think about this is that the population of U.S. college students is now broken into ","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"GtZQRohnO3"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"4 sub-populations","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"D1HAfiXs7Z"}],"key":"HipgchpzsE"},{"type":"text","value":": freshmen, sophomores, juniors, and seniors. Within each of these four groups, we are interested in the GPA.","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"pdo1i52Jiu"}],"key":"BcBW701nNx"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The inference must therefore involve the 4 sub-population means:","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"gxUkmFupb3"}],"key":"TRi1X0iqG6"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineMath","value":"\\mu_1","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_1</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;v